You are editing the highest-risk area of FlashCard AI.

DATA LAYER TRUTH
- Current repo uses EF Core with SQLite by default and PostgreSQL optionally.
- Do not assume SQL Server types, syntax, packages, or migration behavior.
- Do not introduce SQL Server-specific guidance unless I explicitly say the project is migrating to SQL Server.

SCHEMA / DATA RULES
- Be very careful with entity changes.
- Check impact on:
  - EF Core model
  - existing database files
  - future migrations
  - sync payloads
  - client DTO decoding
  - OpenAPI
- Do not change EnsureCreated / migration behavior casually.
- Prefer a staged, explicit migration strategy for production-facing data changes.
- Avoid destructive schema changes unless explicitly requested.
- Avoid raw SQL unless clearly justified.
- Never build SQL from string concatenation with user input.

SYNC RULES
- Preserve user isolation and ownership checks.
- Preserve soft-delete and updated_at semantics.
- Do not silently change merge behavior.
- Call out conflict-resolution behavior explicitly.
- Guest mode must block user-owned push/sync writes.
- Registration/login flow may later require controlled local-to-server migration.

AI / CACHE RULES
- Do not log OpenAI keys, prompt bodies, or user-sensitive content.
- Keep response contracts stable.
- Preferred future direction:
  - server-side translation/audio cache
  - cache key based on normalized text + source language + target language
  - repeated requests should reuse cached translation/audio when appropriate
- Do not break current API shape while adding cache behavior.

AUTH RULES
- Treat JWT, Apple auth, Google auth, and token storage as security-critical.
- Do not rename token fields or storage keys casually.
- Do not add insecure fallback behavior.

OPENAPI RULES
- contracts/openapi/openapi.yaml is a contract artifact.
- When endpoints or DTOs change, update OpenAPI or explicitly state why not.